# Copy to .env before `docker compose up -d`.
#
# NOTHING HERE IS REQUIRED TO START. The broker is secured by an ACCOUNT you
# create on first visit to the dashboard -- see "First run" in docs/operations.md
# -- and the schema creates itself on first connection. The variables below are
# for the cases that need more than that.

# WHERE THE CAMPAIGN LIVES.
# A `postgres://` or `postgresql://` DSN opens Postgres; anything else is a
# SQLite file path. Under compose the default is /data/campaign.sqlite, which is
# a real bind mount and genuinely persists. On a platform with no persistent disk
# (Render, Fly) a SQLite path is silently discarded on every redeploy -- point
# this at Postgres there, and `casebroker doctor` will say so if you have not.
#CASEBROKER_DB=postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<pw>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres

# THE FIRST-RUN GUARD.
# /v1/auth/setup creates the first admin account, and it cannot require a login
# -- there is nobody to log in as yet. So it instead demands whatever credential
# this deployment already holds: this variable if set, otherwise one of
# CASEBROKER_WRITE_TOKENS below. Only a broker with NEITHER is open to whoever
# reaches the form first, so set this before such a host becomes reachable.
# A laptop, or a host behind a firewall, needs neither.
#   uv run casebroker token new --quiet
#CASEBROKER_SETUP_TOKEN=

# THE OLD SHARED-SECRET MODEL, still honoured.
# These predate accounts and per-machine worker tokens. They still work, and the
# live fleet still runs on one, so they are not going anywhere -- but a new
# deployment does not need them, and a new MACHINE should get its own credential
# from the dashboard (Machines > Issue token) rather than a copy of a shared one.
#
#   CASEBROKER_WRITE_TOKENS  read AND write -- lease, heartbeat, complete, fail,
#                            release, add cases.
#   CASEBROKER_READ_TOKENS   read ONLY -- status, case list, one case. Rejected
#                            with 401 by every mutating endpoint.
#
# Each is a comma-separated list, which is how you rotate without stranding a
# worker mid-lease: add the new token, move the workers over, then drop the old.
#CASEBROKER_WRITE_TOKENS=
#CASEBROKER_READ_TOKENS=

# Deprecated spellings, still honoured so existing deployments keep working:
#   CASEBROKER_TOKENS           -> CASEBROKER_WRITE_TOKENS
#   CASEBROKER_READONLY_TOKENS  -> CASEBROKER_READ_TOKENS
# Setting a variable and its deprecated twin to DIFFERENT values is refused at
# startup rather than resolved by precedence -- otherwise a token you believe you
# revoked can quietly keep working.

# WHAT AUTH IS ON. `casebroker health --broker URL` answers it from outside, and
# /healthz reports one of three postures:
#   "token"     one or more env tokens configured
#   "accounts"  no env tokens, but an account exists -- the from-scratch path
#   "OPEN"      neither; every caller has full access. A laptop smoke test only.

# Caddy needs this to get a certificate. It is the one value with no default.
BROKER_DOMAIN=broker.example.org

# -- Site preview: memory ceilings -------------------------------------------
# GET /v1/cases/{id}/footprints reads GeoParquet with DuckDB and two COGs with
# GDAL. Both libraries size their caches from /proc/meminfo, which inside a
# container reports the HOST's memory and not the cgroup limit the platform
# enforces -- DuckDB defaulted to a 51 GiB memory_limit and 12 threads inside a
# far smaller web instance, never spilled, and got OOM-killed. These are the
# explicit ceilings; the defaults suit a small instance and only need raising if
# a dense site is slow on a big one.
#CASEBROKER_DUCKDB_MEMORY=128MB
#CASEBROKER_DUCKDB_THREADS=2
#CASEBROKER_GDAL_CACHE_MB=48

# How many password verifications may run at once. scrypt is ~16 MB a call by
# design, uvicorn's threadpool is 40 wide, and /v1/auth/login needs no
# credential -- so unbounded, 40 simultaneous attempts ask for ~640 MB on a
# 512 MB instance. Four caps it near 64 MB. Raise it on a bigger instance if
# logins queue noticeably.
#CASEBROKER_PASSWORD_CONCURRENCY=4

# How many site previews may run at once. The DuckDB and GDAL ceilings above are
# per CALL, so concurrent previews multiply them rather than sharing them: two at
# once already asks for more than a 512 MB instance has. A queued-out request
# gets 503 + Retry-After rather than waiting forever.
#CASEBROKER_GEO_CONCURRENCY=1
#CASEBROKER_GEO_QUEUE_SECONDS=90

# How many sync request handlers may run at once. anyio defaults to 40, which is
# sized for a machine rather than for a 512 MB instance, and buys nothing here
# because db._LOCK serialises the database work those handlers do anyway.
#CASEBROKER_REQUEST_CONCURRENCY=12

# Fall back to Overture when GlobalBuildingAtlas is unreachable. OFF by default:
# it shells out to a second Python interpreter that loads pyarrow and
# materialises a whole GeoJSON, which is the one part of this path the ceilings
# above cannot reach. Turn it on deliberately, while the GBA mirror is down.
# Requires the optional extra: `pip install casebroker[overture]`. It is not in
# the base install because its client pulls pyarrow -- 122 MB of a 328 MB
# site-packages -- for a path that is off by default.
#CASEBROKER_OVERTURE_FALLBACK=1
